#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b555bf5-8563-5912-b8aa-5092cc5c2456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339080} {:answer/id #uuid "957726a9-5399-5e2d-93c2-57d65d3a747c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339081} {:answer/id #uuid "96f62f42-15f4-507d-acec-0be367977493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339082} {:answer/id #uuid "de439b4c-1956-592d-9b4c-7639a4abeca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339083} {:answer/id #uuid "9cba12c4-5017-5926-9ef7-0c178b5b1715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de439b4c-1956-592d-9b4c-7639a4abeca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339083} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd69973-65c9-51e4-97b5-0155ee907745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c2b1c78-5509-5e6f-b887-46bb53bd6ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337721" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{(1-t)(2-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-3+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$"}]}
